<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">
  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>net11.0</TargetFramework>
    <RootNamespace>Arronix.Format.Video</RootNamespace>
    <AssemblyName>Arronix.Format.Video</AssemblyName>
    <Version>0.1.0</Version>
    <Description>The video format domain: the representation and quality facts a Release&lt;Video&gt; carries. This is the shared, shipped-once assembly every video media kind and every provider that supplies representation facts compiles against.</Description>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <LangVersion>latest</LangVersion>
    <EnforceCodeStyleInBuild>true</EnforceCodeStyleInBuild>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
       The clean semantic surface of the video package, and the one a media declaration or a provider
       reaches for. It holds video's owner semantics: the representation a release carries, the format
       family a media type names in its constructor, and the release preferences video contributes to a
       dependant's compiled policy. All of that is deterministic in-memory domain logic over values.

       What it does not hold is anything with a lifecycle: no recognition vocabulary, no parser, no module,
       no registration execution. Those live in Arronix.Format.Video.Contributions, which references this
       and never the reverse.

       This assembly is admitted once per installation into a Host-owned collectible context and released
       only once every dependant has withdrawn, so its release cadence belongs to the dependency graph
       rather than to this package. That is why it references the universal contracts and nothing else -
       anything it referenced would have to resolve to one copy too - and why it declares no MediaType and
       takes no analyzer: a definition moved here would put its generated reader delegates on that shared
       cadence.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Arronix.Abstractions\Arronix.Abstractions.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>
</Project>
